1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,194
A foreign key is a column that says: this value already exists over there.

2
00:00:05,194 --> 00:00:09,250
orders.customer_id is not a random number.

3
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:13,125
It is a promise that customers has that customer_id.

4
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:17,472
Without the promise, you can insert an order for customer ninety-nine.

5
00:00:17,472 --> 00:00:20,847
Nobody named ninety-nine lives in customers.

6
00:00:20,847 --> 00:00:22,375
The order is an orphan.

7
00:00:22,375 --> 00:00:27,055
Reports that join the two tables will drop it, or invent a blank name.

8
00:00:27,055 --> 00:00:30,000
The foreign key stops the orphan at the door.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,472
Draw two boxes.

10
00:00:31,472 --> 00:00:34,013
customers holds one row per person.

11
00:00:34,013 --> 00:00:36,500
orders holds one row per purchase.

12
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:38,444
The arrow starts on orders.

13
00:00:38,444 --> 00:00:42,000
It lands on customers.customer_id.

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,861
The many side holds the copy of the key.

15
00:00:44,861 --> 00:00:47,138
The one side holds the original.

16
00:00:47,138 --> 00:00:50,250
That direction is easy to get backwards.

17
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:53,666
You do not put order_id on customers.

18
00:00:53,666 --> 00:00:57,013
One customer would then have room for only one order.

19
00:00:57,013 --> 00:01:02,652
You put customer_id on orders, as many times as that person buys.

20
00:01:02,652 --> 00:01:06,625
The parent table is the one whose primary key is copied.

21
00:01:06,625 --> 00:01:09,625
The child table is the one that stores the copy.

22
00:01:09,625 --> 00:01:14,625
Declare the foreign key when you create the child, or add it later with ALTER.

23
00:01:14,625 --> 00:01:18,847
The engine then checks every INSERT and UPDATE on the child.

24
00:01:18,847 --> 00:01:21,958
It also checks DELETE and UPDATE on the parent.

25
00:01:21,958 --> 00:01:27,722
You cannot delete Maya while her orders still point at her — unless you said so on purpose.

26
00:01:27,722 --> 00:01:30,569
That next lesson is foreign key actions.

27
00:01:30,569 --> 00:01:35,527
For now, the default is: refuse the change that would orphan a row.

28
00:01:35,527 --> 00:01:36,958
Types must match.

29
00:01:36,958 --> 00:01:38,500
Integer to integer.

30
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:39,819
Text to text.

31
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:44,097
A value that is allowed to be NULL is a child with no parent yet.

32
00:01:44,097 --> 00:01:46,638
That is a choice, not an accident.

33
00:01:46,638 --> 00:01:52,916
If every order must belong to someone, mark customer_id NOT NULL as well.

34
00:01:52,916 --> 00:01:56,777
The foreign key and NOT NULL are two different promises.

35
00:01:56,777 --> 00:02:01,083
One says: if a value is present, it is a real parent.

36
00:02:01,083 --> 00:02:04,152
The other says: a value must be present.

37
00:02:04,152 --> 00:02:08,138
Name the constraint so error messages read like English.

38
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:13,250
orders_customer_id_fk tells you which door failed.

39
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:16,069
A table may have more than one foreign key.

40
00:02:16,069 --> 00:02:19,333
An order might point at a customer and at a store.

41
00:02:19,333 --> 00:02:21,180
Each arrow is its own rule.

42
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:25,347
Do not store the customer's name on the order just to avoid the key.

43
00:02:25,347 --> 00:02:27,069
That copy will drift.

44
00:02:27,069 --> 00:02:29,569
Let the key find the name when you join.

45
00:02:29,569 --> 00:02:33,833
If you cannot name the parent table, you do not have a foreign key yet.

46
00:02:33,833 --> 00:02:35,611
You have a lonely integer.

47
00:02:35,611 --> 00:02:39,416
Draw the boxes and the arrow before you write the constraint.

48
00:02:39,416 --> 00:02:42,069
The arrow always starts on the many side.

49
00:02:42,069 --> 00:02:45,097
Then the two tables stay related on purpose.

50
00:02:45,097 --> 00:02:48,708
Enable foreign keys in SQLite explicitly.

51
00:02:48,708 --> 00:02:51,708
PRAGMA foreign_keys equals ON.

52
00:02:51,708 --> 00:02:54,222
Otherwise the constraint is jewelry.

53
00:02:54,222 --> 00:03:03,486
PostgreSQL and MySQL enforce them when the engine is asked to, with engine-specific caveats on MySQL's older types.

54
00:03:03,486 --> 00:03:05,041
Know whether yours is on.

55
00:03:05,041 --> 00:03:08,527
A composite foreign key copies more than one column.

56
00:03:08,527 --> 00:03:12,541
Both columns together must match a unique pair on the parent.

57
00:03:12,541 --> 00:03:14,958
Partial copies are not a foreign key.

58
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:16,972
Self-references are allowed.

59
00:03:16,972 --> 00:03:22,208
employee.manager_id points at employee.employee_id.

60
00:03:22,208 --> 00:03:26,680
The top manager is NULL, or points at themselves, by policy.

61
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,263
Cycles in deletes then need a plan.

62
00:03:29,263 --> 00:03:34,291
Deferring a check until the end of a transaction is a PostgreSQL tool.

63
00:03:34,291 --> 00:03:39,402
It lets you insert parent and child in an awkward order inside one BEGIN.

64
00:03:39,402 --> 00:03:41,111
Do not need it on day one.

65
00:03:41,111 --> 00:03:44,083
Know it exists when a loader fails in the middle.

66
00:03:44,083 --> 00:03:48,194
Orphan rows that already exist will block adding the constraint.

67
00:03:48,194 --> 00:03:52,208
Clean them, or map them to a real parent, then ALTER.

68
00:03:52,208 --> 00:03:53,791
The error is a gift.

69
00:03:53,791 --> 00:03:55,722
It named the lie in the data.

70
00:03:55,722 --> 00:04:00,027
Indexes on the child column make the check and the join cheaper.

71
00:04:00,027 --> 00:04:02,333
Add them when the table is not tiny.

72
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:05,944
A foreign key is documentation the engine reads.

73
00:04:05,944 --> 00:04:09,375
Comments in SQL are documentation it does not.

74
00:04:09,375 --> 00:04:12,069
Prefer the one that refuses a bad row.

75
00:04:12,069 --> 00:04:16,611
When two teams share a database, the constraint is the contract.

76
00:04:16,611 --> 00:04:18,916
Meetings do not survive a deploy.

77
00:04:18,916 --> 00:04:20,277
The arrow does.

78
00:04:20,277 --> 00:04:24,125
Draw it, name it, turn it on, and leave it on.

79
00:04:24,125 --> 00:04:26,611
Then joins have something true to match.

80
00:04:26,611 --> 00:04:33,250
Related tables are tables that share a column on purpose, so a row over here can point at a row over there.

81
00:04:33,250 --> 00:04:36,625
The shared column is not a coincidence of names.

82
00:04:36,625 --> 00:04:40,236
It is a meaning: this review is about that product.

83
00:04:40,346 --> 00:04:44,129
In the parent table, that column is the primary key.

84
00:04:44,129 --> 00:04:47,559
In the child table, the copy is the foreign key.

85
00:04:47,559 --> 00:04:50,032
products holds one row per product.

86
00:04:50,032 --> 00:04:52,559
reviews holds one row per review.

87
00:04:52,559 --> 00:04:57,532
reviews.product_id stores which product the review is about.

88
00:04:57,532 --> 00:05:03,254
When both rows show product_id three, the review belongs to that product.

89
00:05:03,254 --> 00:05:11,323
That is the whole trick of related tables: the same identifier in two places, with one place owning the original.

90
00:05:11,323 --> 00:05:15,712
You do not store the product name on every review to save a lookup.

91
00:05:15,712 --> 00:05:19,573
The name would drift the first time marketing renamed the product.

92
00:05:19,573 --> 00:05:23,073
You store the key, and you look the name up when you need it.

93
00:05:23,073 --> 00:05:25,657
The many side holds the foreign key.

94
00:05:25,657 --> 00:05:28,365
The one side holds the primary key.

95
00:05:28,365 --> 00:05:34,282
If you put review_id on products, a product could remember only one review.

96
00:05:34,282 --> 00:05:39,573
That is the backwards arrow, and it shows up as soon as a second review arrives.

97
00:05:39,573 --> 00:05:44,490
Declare the foreign key in CREATE TABLE, or add it later with ALTER.

98
00:05:44,490 --> 00:05:51,323
Name the constraint so the error reads like English: reviews_product_id_fk.

99
00:05:51,323 --> 00:05:54,948
The engine then checks INSERT and UPDATE on the child.

100
00:05:54,948 --> 00:06:00,393
You cannot insert a review for product ninety-nine if products has no ninety-nine.

101
00:06:00,393 --> 00:06:03,921
That refused insert is an orphan you did not create.

102
00:06:03,921 --> 00:06:07,671
The engine also checks DELETE and UPDATE on the parent.

103
00:06:07,671 --> 00:06:14,421
You cannot delete a product while reviews still point at it, unless you declared an action that says otherwise.

104
00:06:14,421 --> 00:06:18,990
The default is: refuse the change that would leave a dangling pointer.

105
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:23,671
Types must match: integer to integer, text to text.

106
00:06:23,671 --> 00:06:27,087
A NULL foreign key is a child with no parent yet.

107
00:06:27,087 --> 00:06:30,393
That is allowed only if you did not also say NOT NULL.

108
00:06:30,393 --> 00:06:36,532
Two promises: if a value is present it is a real parent, and a value must be present.

109
00:06:36,532 --> 00:06:39,504
A table may hold more than one foreign key.

110
00:06:39,504 --> 00:06:43,004
A review may point at a product and at a user.

111
00:06:43,004 --> 00:06:45,809
Each arrow is its own named constraint.

112
00:06:45,809 --> 00:06:48,629
Insert parents first, then children.

113
00:06:48,629 --> 00:06:54,643
Delete children first, then parents — unless an ON DELETE action does that work for you.

114
00:06:54,643 --> 00:06:58,754
In SQLite, foreign keys are off until you turn them on.

115
00:06:58,754 --> 00:07:01,754
PRAGMA foreign_keys equals ON.

116
00:07:01,754 --> 00:07:04,365
Without it, the constraint is jewelry.

117
00:07:04,365 --> 00:07:11,032
A composite foreign key copies more than one column, and the parent must have a matching composite key.

118
00:07:11,032 --> 00:07:14,129
Do not invent a shared column that is not a key.

119
00:07:14,129 --> 00:07:17,809
A city name in two tables is not a relationship.

120
00:07:17,809 --> 00:07:20,907
It is a duplicated fact waiting to disagree.

121
00:07:20,907 --> 00:07:25,004
If you cannot name the parent table, you do not have a foreign key.

122
00:07:25,004 --> 00:07:26,782
You have a lonely integer.

123
00:07:26,782 --> 00:07:30,476
Give it a parent, or stop pretending the tables are related.

124
00:07:30,476 --> 00:07:36,212
Related tables formally: tables connected by one or more common key columns.

125
00:07:36,212 --> 00:07:38,698
Not common names, key columns.

126
00:07:38,698 --> 00:07:43,768
Product_id is a key on products and a foreign key on reviews.

127
00:07:43,768 --> 00:07:48,226
Manufacturer as text on two tables is not relatedness.

128
00:07:48,226 --> 00:07:52,059
Manage data in related tables by respecting the parent.

129
00:07:52,059 --> 00:08:01,254
You cannot load reviews.csv first if products is empty, unless you defer constraints, which you should not do as a beginner habit.

130
00:08:01,254 --> 00:08:03,532
Load parents, then children.

131
00:08:03,532 --> 00:08:10,504
Or load into staging tables without foreign keys, clean orphans, then INSERT into the real child.

132
00:08:10,504 --> 00:08:17,101
That staging pattern is still the same idea: the parent must exist before the pointer is honest.

133
00:08:17,101 --> 00:08:23,018
When two tables share a column that is not a key, fix the design before you write the join.

134
00:08:23,018 --> 00:08:24,323
The join will run.

135
00:08:24,323 --> 00:08:25,726
The meaning will not.

136
00:08:25,726 --> 00:08:29,365
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

137
00:08:29,365 --> 00:08:32,421
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

138
00:08:32,421 --> 00:08:34,504
What makes two tables related?

139
00:08:34,504 --> 00:08:39,712
A key column copied on purpose, parent as primary, child as foreign.

140
00:08:39,712 --> 00:08:41,573
Where does the foreign key live?

141
00:08:41,573 --> 00:08:43,796
On the many side, the child.

142
00:08:43,796 --> 00:08:47,323
What happens if you insert a child with a fake parent id?

143
00:08:47,323 --> 00:08:50,476
The engine refuses, if the constraint is on.

144
00:08:50,476 --> 00:08:54,365
What happens if you delete a parent with children and no action?

145
00:08:54,365 --> 00:08:56,615
The engine refuses the delete.

146
00:08:56,615 --> 00:08:59,240
Is a shared city name a relationship?

147
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:00,004
No.

148
00:09:00,004 --> 00:09:01,976
It is a duplicated fact.

149
00:09:01,976 --> 00:09:04,434
Can one child have two foreign keys?

150
00:09:04,434 --> 00:09:05,337
Yes.

151
00:09:05,337 --> 00:09:08,434
A review can point at a product and a user.

152
00:09:08,434 --> 00:09:09,823
Must types match?

153
00:09:09,823 --> 00:09:10,726
Yes.

154
00:09:10,726 --> 00:09:13,559
Integer to integer, text to text.

155
00:09:13,559 --> 00:09:15,657
What does a NULL foreign key mean?

156
00:09:15,657 --> 00:09:20,421
A child with no parent yet, only legal if NOT NULL is absent.

157
00:09:20,546 --> 00:09:22,426
Which insert order is safe?

158
00:09:22,426 --> 00:09:24,732
Parents first, then children.

159
00:09:24,732 --> 00:09:28,065
Which delete order is safe under the default action?

160
00:09:28,065 --> 00:09:30,537
Children first, then parents.

161
00:09:30,537 --> 00:09:32,093
Why name the constraint?

162
00:09:32,093 --> 00:09:34,579
So the error names the door that failed.

163
00:09:34,579 --> 00:09:38,121
Does SQLite enforce foreign keys by default?

164
00:09:38,121 --> 00:09:38,884
No.

165
00:09:38,884 --> 00:09:39,912
Turn them on.

166
00:09:39,912 --> 00:09:43,148
Is product name on the review a substitute for a key?

167
00:09:43,148 --> 00:09:43,912
No.

168
00:09:43,912 --> 00:09:45,412
The name will drift.

169
00:09:45,412 --> 00:09:46,621
What is an orphan?

170
00:09:46,621 --> 00:09:48,968
A child pointer with no parent row.

171
00:09:48,968 --> 00:09:50,496
What is the parent table?

172
00:09:50,496 --> 00:09:53,440
The table whose primary key is copied.

173
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:55,259
When do you add the foreign key?

174
00:09:55,259 --> 00:09:58,440
At CREATE of the child, or later with ALTER.

175
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:00,634
Can a foreign key be composite?

176
00:10:00,634 --> 00:10:03,926
Yes, if the parent key is composite too.

177
00:10:03,926 --> 00:10:06,634
Should application code be the only check?

178
00:10:06,634 --> 00:10:07,398
No.

179
00:10:07,398 --> 00:10:09,621
A second writer will skip your code.

180
00:10:09,621 --> 00:10:11,926
What is the default action in words?

181
00:10:11,926 --> 00:10:14,551
Refuse the change that would orphan a row.

182
00:10:14,551 --> 00:10:15,843
How do you verify?

183
00:10:15,843 --> 00:10:19,357
Insert a fake parent id and watch the refusal.

184
00:10:19,357 --> 00:10:24,079
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

